<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vytautas the Great</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1350–<a href="/years/1430">1430</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Vytautas the Great of Lithuania, Witold</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vytautas the Great was Grand Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> from 1392 to 1430, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kestutis">Kęstutis</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trakai">Trakai</a> and cousin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a>. He led Lithuanian forces with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> to victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> at Grunwald in 1410, extended the grand duchy towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, and settled Karaites and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> at Trakai.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vytautas-the-great.jpg" alt="Vytautas the Great" width="320" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_Vytautas_the_Great.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Even so, the emir Edigu crushed <b>Vytautas of Lithuania</b> at the Battle of the Vorskla River in 1399 and besieged Moscow in 1408.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Vytautas</b> granted privileges to the Jews in 1388 and settled Tatars and Karaites near Trakai, communities whose descendants still live in Lithuania.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Order's military reputation collapsed at the Battle of Grunwald (Tannenberg) on 15 July 1410, when a Polish-Lithuanian army under Władysław II Jagiełło and <b>Vytautas</b> killed Grand Master Ulrich von Jungingen and much of the Order's leadership.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>On an islet of the Neman River, <b>Vytautas the Great</b>, ruler of Lithuania, and Grand Master Konrad von Jungingen signed the Treaty of Salynas, promising Samogitia to the Teutonic Knights for the third time.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-12">12 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1393 <b>Vytautas</b> expelled Fyodor Koriatovich and divided the duchy, with the western part passing to Poland.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-podolia">Duchy of Podolia</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1395 <b>Vytautas</b> removed Volodymyr Olgerdovich from the Principality of Kiev and gave him Slutsk and Kapyl in compensation.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-slutsk">Duchy of Slutsk</a></li></ul></section>
